# Data access

Reads and writes of `decks` and `cards` stay on the server. A Client Component
never queries the database, never calls `fetch` for app data, and never talks
to Drizzle. [src/index.ts](mdc:src/index.ts) is the seed script only; it is the
one place a write may run outside a Server Action.

Ownership still comes from `auth()` as described in
[.cursor/rules/clerk.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/clerk.mdc). Zod checks the shape of
input. It does not decide who owns a row.

## Reads

Load `decks` and `cards` in Server Components (pages, layouts, or async
server-only helpers they call). Pass the result into Client Components as
props. Do not load this data from a Client Component, a Route Handler, or a
Server Action whose only job is to return rows.

## Writes

Every `insert`, `update`, and `delete` of `decks` or `cards` runs inside a
Server Action. Put `'use server'` at the top of the action file. Do not mutate
those tables from a Route Handler, a Server Component render, or client code.
Queries themselves still follow
[.cursor/rules/drizzle.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/drizzle.mdc).

## Validation

Validate with Zod from the `zod` package. Do not hand-check fields with
`if` statements, and do not trust values because TypeScript already describes
them.

Every value passed into a Server Action has a TypeScript type derived from its
Zod schema. The action parameter is that type. It is never `FormData`, and the
action does not read fields with `formData.get`. A Client Component builds a
plain object and passes that object.

Parse the argument with `safeParse` before any write. Use `parsed.data` for the
query. When parsing fails, return the validation error and do not write.

```ts
'use server'

import { z } from 'zod'

const createDeckSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().trim().min(1),
  description: z.string().trim().optional(),
})

type CreateDeckInput = z.infer<typeof createDeckSchema>

export async function createDeck(input: CreateDeckInput) {
  const parsed = createDeckSchema.safeParse(input)
  if (!parsed.success) {
    return { error: parsed.error.flatten() }
  }

  const { name, description } = parsed.data
  // insert name and description for the signed-in user
}
```
